/-!
# Lagrange's theorem

Let $`G` be a finite group and $`H` a subgroup. The distinct left cosets of $`H`
partition $`G`, and each coset has $`|H|` elements. If $`[G:H]` denotes the number
of left cosets, then $`|G|=[G:H]\,|H|`. In particular, $`|H|` divides $`|G|`.

In our example modulo 12, there are three cosets, each of size four, so
$`12=3\cdot 4`. Which part of the proof guarantees that each residue gets
counted exactly once? Which part gives the four elements in each coset?

## From a partition to a product

Choose one representative $`g_C` for each coset $`C`. Every element $`x` of
$`G` belongs to a unique coset $`C` and can be written uniquely as $`g_C h`
with $`h\in H`. The pair $`(C,h)` records its coset and its position in that
coset. The inverse map takes $`(C,h)` to $`g_C h`.

The library result {name}`Subgroup.groupEquivQuotientProdSubgroup` packages this
bijection as `G ≃ (G ⧸ H) × H`. Here `G ⧸ H` is the type of left cosets,
and `Nat.card (G ⧸ H)` is the index. We will use the supplied bijection to
prove the counting formula.
-/

namespace cx_2026

variable {G : Type*} [Group G] (H : Subgroup G)

NewTactic exact rw have use sorry calc
NewTheorem Nat.card_prod Nat.card_congr Nat.mul_comm Exists.intro

